$?","options":["$0$ and $1$.","$A$ and $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ only.","$\\varnothing$ and $A$.","Only the one-element subsets of $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a7042372603109f4dc74d9bf","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If a finite set has $n$ elements, how many elements does its power set have?","options":["$2^n$","$n+2$","$2n$","$n^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3c94483b942622936a8464a9","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $\\varnothing\\in\\mathcal{P}(A)$ true for every set $A$?","options":["Because $\\varnothing\\subseteq A$ for every set $A$.","Because the power set excludes nonempty subsets.","Because $A\\subseteq\\varnothing$ for every set $A$.","Because $\\varnothing$ is always an element of $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bc7ff55427ebeef6e9679c3a","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the power set $\\mathcal{P}(\\varnothing)$?","options":["It contains every finite set.","It has exactly one element, namely $\\varnothing$.","It has two elements.","It is equal to $\\varnothing$ itself."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac558fe5bde7838d7410c8f8","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\{a,b\\}$, which is $\\mathcal{P}(A)$?","options":["$\\{\\varnothing,\\{a\\},\\{b\\},\\{a,b\\}\\}$","$\\{\\varnothing,a,b,\\{a,b\\}\\}$","$\\{\\{a\\},\\{b\\}\\}$","$\\{a,b,\\varnothing\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0382ab514d777462fe148b9e","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$, what is $|\\mathcal{P}(A)|$?","options":["$9$","$3$","$8$","$6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"37c7d40ec3a7da972185da70","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\{1,2\\}$, which object is an element of $\\mathcal{P}(A)$?","options":["$3$","$\\{1,3\\}$","$\\{1\\}$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"693eb866e31329a9f73a1460","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\{1,2\\}$, why is $1\\notin\\mathcal{P}(A)$?","options":["Because $1$ is an element of $A$, not a subset of $A$.","Because $1$ is a subset of $A$, not an element.","Because power sets contain only numbers larger than $2$.","Because $\\{1\\}$ is not a subset of $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b31209b184e956462d771b8f","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\mathcal{P}(\\{\\varnothing\\})$?","options":["$\\{\\varnothing\\}$","$\\{\\varnothing,\\{\\varnothing\\}\\}$","$\\varnothing$","$\\{\\{\\varnothing\\}\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0580b40c1e3b9dea47e294b8","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the notation $2^A$ natural for a power set?","options":["Each subset of $A$ is either empty or all of $A$","The power set has $2\\cdot|A|$ elements, since each one is in or out","Each element of $A$ is either in a subset or not: two choices","$2^A$ collects the pairs of elements of $A$: the subsets of size two"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e893697af4462adbd90f2b25","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does Cantor's theorem say about the size of $\\mathcal{P}(A)$?","options":["It always has the same cardinality as $A$.","It is smaller than $A$ whenever $A$ is infinite.","It has larger cardinality only when $A$ is finite.","It has strictly larger cardinality than $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"df0e4a1b7a18c3ddf72a964d","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Is $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ usually a subset of $A$?","options":["No. Its elements are subsets of $A$, not usually elements of $A$.","No, because $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ never contains $A$.","Yes. Every power set is contained in the original set.","Yes, but only when $A$ is finite."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"473dd28a941de7fff2a2b6e7","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ is finite and $|\\mathcal{P}(A)|=16$, what is $|A|$?","options":["$8$","$16$","$4$","$15$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"318e304e93d2b8a1a8d97fe1","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which subset of $\\{x,y,z\\}$ has exactly two elements?","options":["$x$","$\\{x,z\\}$","$\\varnothing$","$\\{x,y,z,0\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fcc07e3e6e6b5bdb5e988cbb","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which set has exactly one element?","options":["$\\mathcal{P}(\\varnothing)$","$\\varnothing$","$\\mathcal{P}(\\{a,b\\})$","$\\{a,b\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5d02677c4597a91ef1aa7c6","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does $A\\in\\mathcal{P}(A)$ hold?","options":["Because $A$ must be empty.","Because the power set contains only proper subsets.","Because every set is an element of itself.","Because every set is a subset of itself."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fe836db282aef842465b6393","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Cantor's theorem gives $|A|<|\\mathcal{P}(A)|$ for every set $A$. Which conclusion does this rule out?","options":["There is no bijection from $A$ onto $\\mathcal{P}(A)$.","There is no subset of $\\mathcal{P}(A)$.","There is no function from $A$ to $\\mathcal{P}(A)$.","There is no injection from $A$ into $\\mathcal{P}(A)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ce45f6c5d9ffa8bd66f539de","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\{0,\\{0\\}\\}$. Which list gives all elements of $\\mathcal{P}(A)$?","options":["$0,\\{0\\},\\{0,\\{0\\}\\}$","$\\varnothing,\\{0\\},\\{\\{0\\}\\},\\{0,\\{0\\}\\}$","$\\varnothing,0,\\{0\\},\\{\\{0\\}\\}$","$\\varnothing,\\{0\\},\\{0,0\\},\\{\\{0,0\\}\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f3ea02e9b0268a92e307a0a6","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch23_power_set_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\{a,b\\}$, what is $|\\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(A))|$?","options":["$4$","$16$","$2$","$8$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d1511f38158336e3813ce669","time_limit":40}]}}